\description{
Evaluates a system of ordinary differential equations separately for
each group of rows and returns the solution aligned with the rows of
the data, so that a compartment model can be written directly in the
body of a \code{bf(..., nl = TRUE)} formula. The dynamics parameters and
the initial states are ordinary nonlinear parameters, which means
they take fixed effects, random effects and covariates like any other
linear predictor, and the Laplace approximation is exact through the
solver's adjoint.
}
\section{What the group is}{
\code{group} names the unit that owns one ODE system: a subject in a
population pharmacokinetic model, a reactor, a patient. Every row of
one group is one observation of that group's trajectory, at the time
given by \code{times}. One solve is performed per group, over that group's
own sorted times, and the results are scattered back into the input
row order. Ragged designs, unsorted rows, repeated times and an
observation at \code{t0} itself are all fine.

Groups are never stacked into one large system. That is a hard
constraint, not an implementation detail: the second-order derivative
path the Laplace approximation needs returns \code{NaN} above roughly
eight states in a single system, so a stacked solve gives silently
wrong or missing gradients. \code{frm_ode()} warns when one system alone
exceeds that many states.
}

\section{What is constant within a group}{
\code{init} and \code{parms} are read off each group's \strong{first row}. A
dynamics parameter must therefore not vary inside a group: a
covariate that changes between an early and a late observation of the
same subject describes a model this helper cannot solve. Such a
covariate is refused, by name, rather than silently ignored.
Covariates that are constant within a group (a subject's weight, dose
or treatment arm) are the intended case and are unrestricted.

\code{tv} is the exception, and the next section is about it.
}

\section{Time-varying inputs}{
\code{tv} carries dynamics inputs that DO change inside a group, as long
as they change in steps: a creatinine clearance measured again at
each visit, a dose-dependent rate that switches at a protocol
amendment, a temperature held at one level and then another. The
solve is split at each change point, exactly as it is split at a dose
time, and each segment's dynamics see that segment's value.

The values follow \code{parms} in the vector \code{dynamics} is handed, so with
\code{parms = list(ka, V)} and \code{tv = list(ke)} the derivative function
reads \code{p[1]}, \code{p[2]} and \code{p[3]}. Nothing in \code{dynamics} learns that
\code{p[3]} ever changed: within one segment it is an ordinary constant,
and because it is a parameter it is a tape input, so an estimated
time-varying value is differentiated exactly.

The step function is \strong{last observation carried forward}, which is
rxode2's convention for covariates. A row's value is in force from
that row's time until the next change, and the first row's value
reaches back to \code{t0}. A consequence worth knowing: the state is
continuous across a change point, because a covariate moves the
derivative and not the state, so an observation exactly at a change
point reads what the PRE-change dynamics produced. The difference
shows up only afterwards. (This is unlike a dose, which jumps the
state, and where the same observation reads the trough.)

The values may be estimated; the change points may not. They decide
where the solve is split, which is settled before the tape is built.
When a \code{tv} column carries estimated values there is nothing to
compare - RTMB refuses comparison on AD types - so \code{tv_break} has to
name the data column whose changes within a group mark them:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{bf(conc ~ frm_ode(pk_dyn, init = list(dose, 0), times = time,
                  parms = list(exp(lka), exp(lV)),
                  tv = list(exp(lke)), tv_break = visit,
                  group = id, output = 2L),
   lka ~ 1, lV ~ 1, lke ~ 1 + crcl, nl = TRUE)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Here \code{lke} is a linear predictor that varies within a subject, which
\code{parms} would refuse. \code{tv_break = visit} says the value is constant
inside a visit, and a plain-numeric \code{tv} column that disagrees with
that is refused. When every \code{tv} column is plain data, \code{tv_break} can
be left out and the change points are read off the columns.

A time-varying input other than a step function is not available. See
"Boundaries".
}

\section{Failed solves}{
\code{on_error} and \code{penalty} reach less than they look like they do, and
the difference matters:
\itemize{
\item \strong{While fitting}, the body is evaluated on the
automatic-differentiation tape. \code{RTMBode::ode()} returns an
\code{advector} and does not raise an R error when the trajectory goes
bad, and a non-finite AD value cannot be tested for (RTMB refuses
comparison on AD types). So a diverging region of the parameter
space is \strong{not} caught here. It surfaces instead as the
optimizer's own \verb{NA/NaN function evaluation} or \verb{NA/NaN gradient evaluation} warning, and \code{on_error = "error"} will not name the
group. The two failures that are still caught on the tape are an
integrator that gives up and returns fewer time points than were
asked for, and any R error raised by \code{dynamics} itself.
\item \strong{Everywhere else} the evaluation is ordinary numeric arithmetic,
and every check applies: \code{\link[=predict]{predict()}}, \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}},
\code{\link[=residuals]{residuals()}}, a direct call, and a fit whose \code{init} and \code{parms}
contain no estimated parameter (which is evaluated once, numerically,
as the tape is built). Here \code{on_error = "penalize"} writes \code{penalty}
into the failed group's rows, and \code{on_error = "error"} stops and
names the group.
}

A penalty is never written silently: \code{frm_ode()} warns, naming the
groups, and \code{\link[=frm_ode_failures]{frm_ode_failures()}} reports them afterwards. Treat those
rows as missing, not as predictions.

If a fit reports \verb{NA/NaN gradient evaluation}, run \code{diagnose()} on it,
then call \code{frm_ode()} directly at the suspect parameter values with
\code{on_error = "error"}: numerically it will name the group that cannot
be solved.

Solver warnings from \pkg{deSolve} ("corrector convergence failed
repeatedly", "exceeded maxsteps") during a fit come from the
optimizer's probing steps and are usually not fatal. Judge the fit by
the gradient at the optimum, not by whether the solver complained.
}

\section{Dosing events}{
\code{events} is a data.frame of doses, one row per dose, with columns:
\itemize{
\item \code{group} (optional): which group the row applies to, matching the
values of \code{group}. Leave the column out and the schedule applies to
every group.
\item \code{time}: when the dose happens. At or after \code{t0}.
\item \code{state}: the state it goes into, by name (requires \code{states}) or by
position, resolved exactly as \code{output} is. Optional for a
one-state system, and ignored on a \code{"reset"} row, which names no
compartment because it sets every one of them.
\item \code{value}: how much.
\item \code{method} (optional, default \code{"add"}): \code{"add"} puts \code{value} into the
state, \code{"replace"} sets the state to \code{value}, \code{"multiply"} scales
it - the \pkg{deSolve} event methods - and \code{"reset"} sets \strong{every}
state to \code{value}.
\item \code{duration} (optional, default \code{0}): a positive value makes the row
an infusion, delivering \code{value} at the constant rate
\code{value / duration} over \verb{[time, time + duration]}. Infusions must
use \code{"add"}.
\item \code{ii} (optional, default \code{0}): the interdose interval, for \code{addl}
and \code{ss}.
\item \code{addl} (optional, default \code{0}): how many further doses follow this
one, at \code{time + ii}, \code{time + 2 * ii}, and so on. The rows are
written out internally, so the table means exactly what the
hand-expanded one means.
\item \code{ss} (optional, default \code{FALSE}): the row's cycle has already
reached steady state. See "Steady-state dosing".
}

Inside a \code{bf(nl = TRUE)} body, name the table:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{doses <- data.frame(time = seq(12, 48, by = 12), state = "depot",
                    value = 100)
conc ~ frm_ode(pk_dyn, ..., events = doses)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A name in a nonlinear body is normally a request for a column of
\code{data}, and a column of that name still wins. A data.frame is not
something a column could hold, so \code{doses} is read from the formula
environment instead. Writing the table \strong{inline}, or holding it in a
function of no arguments, works the same way and is what a schedule
read at fit time wants:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{conc ~ frm_ode(pk_dyn, ..., events = data.frame(
         time = seq(12, 48, by = 12), state = "depot", value = 100))

schedule <- function() read.csv("doses.csv")
conc ~ frm_ode(pk_dyn, ..., events = schedule)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

In NONMEM terms an \code{"add"} row is a dosing record (\code{evid = 1}) with
\code{amt = value} into \code{cmt = state}; a row with \code{duration} is the same
record with \code{rate = amt / duration}; \code{ii} and \code{addl} are spelled the
same way there and in rxode2. A \code{"reset"} row is \code{evid = 3}, which
both NONMEM and rxode2 read as "set every compartment to zero and
carry on" - write \code{value = 0} for that reading. \code{evid = 4}, a reset
followed by a dose, is a \code{"reset"} row and an \code{"add"} row at the same
time; the reset is always applied first, so the order of the two rows
in the table does not matter.

\code{frm_ode()} does not read NONMEM column names, and there is no \code{evid}
column: observation rows are the rows of \code{data}, and dose rows are
the rows of \code{events}, which is a separate table. A NONMEM-shaped
dataset has to be split into the two.

An observation at exactly a dose time reads the state \strong{before} the
dose, which is the trough, matching both the \pkg{deSolve} convention
and the usual reading of a pre-dose sample. That includes an
observation at \code{t0} with a dose at \code{t0}: it reads \code{init}. The one
exception is an \code{ss} row, which is not a jump from anything.
}

\section{Steady-state dosing}{
\code{ss = TRUE} on an \code{events} row says the system has already been given
this dose every \code{ii} for long enough to settle. It is reached by
simulation, not by a closed form, so it works for any dynamics:
every compartment is set to zero (NONMEM's and rxode2's reading of a
steady-state record, and the reason an \code{ss} row overrides \code{init}),
and the cycle is then repeated \code{n_ss} times before the record's time.
The record's own dose is applied afterwards, so an observation at the
record's time reads the steady-state \strong{trough}.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# 100 into the depot every 12 hours, already at steady state at t = 0
data.frame(time = 0, state = "depot", value = 100, ii = 12, ss = TRUE)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\code{n_ss} is the honest part of this. A real steady state is the limit
of repeating until the cycle-start state stops moving, and that test
branches on a value, which the tape cannot do: the number of solves
has to be fixed before the tape is built. So \code{n_ss} cycles is an
\strong{approximation}, and its error is geometric - for linear kinetics
the shortfall after \code{n} cycles is the accumulation factor to the
power \code{n}, which is \code{exp(-n * k * ii)} for a one-compartment system.
At the default \code{n_ss = 20} that is \code{1e-21} for a drug eliminated over
its dosing interval and only a percent or two for one whose
half-life is many intervals long. Off the tape - a direct call,
\code{\link[=predict]{predict()}}, \code{\link[=simulate]{simulate()}}, a body holding no estimated parameter -
the last two cycles are compared and \code{frm_ode()} warns when they
still differ by more than \code{ss_tol}. During a fit that check cannot
run, so read the warning from a numeric call and raise \code{n_ss} if it
fires.

The cost is \code{n_ss} extra solves per group (two per cycle for an
infusion), so a steady-state population fit is several times a plain
one. One \code{ss} row per group is allowed; write later doses out with
\code{ii} and \code{addl}.

The doses are not handed to \pkg{deSolve} as events. \code{frm_ode()}
splits the integration at the event times and chains one solve per
interval, carrying the state across the break itself. That is a
correctness requirement, not a style choice: \pkg{RTMBode} solves an
augmented system carrying the derivatives of the states with respect
to the parameters, and a \pkg{deSolve} event jumps the state without
jumping those derivatives, which gives a wrong gradient for
\code{"replace"} and \code{"multiply"} (measured at 42\% and 59\% relative error).
Splitting the solve is exact for every method. It costs one solve per
dosing interval per group, so an intensively dosed design is
proportionally slower.
}

\section{Doses that depend on a parameter}{
\code{events} is data: \code{value} is a numeric column, so it cannot hold an
estimated quantity. \code{event_scale} is the way in. It is one value per
observation (constant within group, like \code{init} and \code{parms}), and it
multiplies the \code{value} of every event in that group, so a
bioavailability written as a nonlinear parameter estimates a dose
scale that carries covariates and random effects like any other:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{frm_ode(pk_dyn, init = list(0, 0), times = time, parms = ...,
        group = id, events = doses, event_scale = plogis(logitF))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Because scaling only makes sense for a dose, \code{event_scale} is refused
on a table containing \code{"replace"} or \code{"multiply"} rows. A \code{"reset"}
row is allowed beside scaled doses and is simply not scaled: it names
a level for the states, not an amount.
}

\section{One solve, a different state per row}{
\code{output} normally selects states. Given one value per row instead, it
says which state THAT row reads, and the whole group still takes one
solve. This is the spelling for a parent and its metabolite reported
in one assay column, or for two species of a predator-prey series
stacked long:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{bf(y ~ frm_ode(pm_dyn, init = list(dose, 0, 0), times = time,
               parms = list(exp(lka), exp(lkm), exp(lke)),
               group = id, output = cmt),          # cmt is 2 or 3
   lka ~ 1, lkm ~ 1, lke ~ 1, nl = TRUE)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The two readings are told apart by length: a per-row selection needs
one value per row AND more rows than the system has states. A
selection of states is shorter than that. The per-row values are
resolved by name or position exactly as a scalar \code{output} is, and
they have to be data - a state index cannot be differentiated.
}

\section{Boundaries}{
Time-varying input has to be piecewise constant, and the reason is
worth knowing. \pkg{RTMBode} tapes \code{dynamics} once, so \code{t} is an
automatic-differentiation value inside it. A branch on time
(\code{if (t < t_end) rate else 0}) raises "Comparison is generally unsafe
for AD types", and an \code{approxfun()} forcing table silently returns
the value at the taping point instead of failing. Smooth arithmetic in
\code{t} is fine. A step function belongs in \code{tv}, or in \code{events} as an
infusion, where it is carried as a parameter over each interval and
differentiated exactly; \pkg{deSolve}'s own \code{forcings} argument is not
reachable, because \pkg{RTMBode}'s compiled derivative shim has no
forcing hook. Linear interpolation between measured covariate values,
which rxode2 offers, is not available: it would need the value inside
the segment to depend on \code{t}.

Estimated event times, lag times, inter-dose intervals and \code{tv}
change points are not supported: they decide where the solve is
split, which is settled before the tape is built.

\code{predict(se.fit = TRUE)} is not available for a nonlinear predictor,
including one containing \code{frm_ode()}; request a nonlinear parameter
with \code{predict(dpar = )} instead.
}

\section{Installation}{

\code{frm_ode()} needs \pkg{RTMBode}, which is not on CRAN:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{install.packages("RTMBode", repos = c(
  "https://kaskr.r-universe.dev",
  "https://cloud.r-project.org"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\examples{
# One-compartment oral pharmacokinetics with between-subject
# variability on the absorption and elimination rates.
#   dA/dt = -ka A            A(0) = dose
#   dC/dt =  ka A / V - ke C C(0) = 0
pk_dyn <- function(t, y, p) {
  list(c(-p[1] * y[1], p[1] * y[1] / p[3] - p[2] * y[2]))
}

set.seed(2026)
tt <- c(0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 12)
n_id <- 6
dd <- data.frame(id = factor(rep(seq_len(n_id), each = length(tt))),
                 time = rep(tt, n_id), dose = 100)
ka <- exp(rnorm(n_id, 0, 0.3))[as.integer(dd$id)]
ke <- exp(rnorm(n_id, log(0.2), 0.25))[as.integer(dd$id)]
dd$conc <- 100 * ka / (10 * (ka - ke)) *
  (exp(-ke * dd$time) - exp(-ka * dd$time)) + rnorm(nrow(dd), 0, 0.3)

if (requireNamespace("RTMBode", quietly = TRUE)) {
  \donttest{
  fit <- frm(
    bf(conc ~ frm_ode(pk_dyn,
                      init   = list(dose, 0),
                      times  = time,
                      parms  = list(exp(lka), exp(lke), exp(lV)),
                      group  = id,
                      states = c("depot", "central"),
                      output = "central"),
       lka ~ 1 + (1 | id), lke ~ 1 + (1 | id), lV ~ 1, nl = TRUE) +
      gaussian(),
    data = dd, start = list(beta = c(0, log(0.25), log(8))))
  fixef(fit)
  }

  # Repeated dosing: 100 into the depot every 12 hours. The dose at
  # time 0 is the initial condition, the rest are events.
  doses <- data.frame(time = c(12, 24, 36), state = "depot",
                      value = 100)
  frm_ode(pk_dyn, init = list(100, 0), times = c(6, 18, 30, 42),
          parms = list(1, 0.2, 10), states = c("depot", "central"),
          output = "central", events = doses)

  # The same schedule written compactly, and already at steady state
  frm_ode(pk_dyn, init = list(0, 0), times = c(6, 18, 30, 42),
          parms = list(1, 0.2, 10), states = c("depot", "central"),
          output = "central",
          events = data.frame(time = 0, state = "depot", value = 100,
                              ii = 12, ss = TRUE))

  # An elimination rate that doubles after hour 6, carried forward
  # from the row it appears on. `tv` values follow `parms`, so this
  # dynamics reads ka at p[1], V at p[2] and the time-varying ke at
  # p[3].
  pk_tv <- function(t, y, p) {
    list(c(-p[1] * y[1], p[1] * y[1] / p[2] - p[3] * y[2]))
  }
  tt <- c(1, 3, 6, 9, 12)
  frm_ode(pk_tv, init = list(100, 0), times = tt, parms = list(1, 10),
          tv = list(ifelse(tt < 6, 0.2, 0.4)),
          states = c("depot", "central"), output = "central")
}
}
